      "claim": "A high-transfer clinical drug measured at 4% absolute oral bioavailability in humans (range 1–9%) provides direct human evidence that this chemical space is associated with F well below 20%.",
      "evidence_role": "supportive"
    },
    {
      "card_ids": ["C17"],
      "claim": "In fasted humans, the high-transfer molecule DX-9065 had only 3% oral bioavailability as a conventional capsule, matching the query's fasted condition and supporting low F.",
      "evidence_role": "supportive"
    },
    {
      "card_ids": ["C20"],
      "claim": "A high-transfer heteroaromatic clinical candidate was discontinued for unexpectedly poor oral bioavailability in humans due to aldehyde oxidase-mediated clearance; the query's nitrogen-rich pyrazolopyrimidine may face analogous clearance liabilities, supporting low F.",
      "evidence_role": "supportive"
    },
    {
      "card_ids": ["C08"],
      "claim": "Melagatran, another high-transfer small-molecule clinical drug, showed only 3–7% oral bioavailability in humans, reinforcing that the high-transfer evidence consistently predicts F below 20%.",
      "evidence_role": "supportive"
    }
  ],
  "confidence": "high",
  "decision_summary": "All high-transfer human evidence consistently shows absolute oral bioavailability below 10% (mostly 2–4%) for molecules in this transfer neighborhood, and the query's nitrogen-rich heteroaromatic/acrylamide structure does not provide a basis to expect F ≥20%. Therefore predict low.",
